## When to panic

```xiom
fn allocate_buffer(size: Int) -> *Int {
  if size <= 0 {
    panic("buffer size must be positive, got: " + size.to_str());
  }
  // Allocation...
}
```

### panic vs assert

| Feature | panic | assert |
|---------|-------|--------|
| Strippable | No (always present) | Yes (--no-asserts) |
| Use case | Unrecoverable runtime errors | Debug-only sanity checks |
| Production | Remains active | Typically stripped |

### Appropriate Uses of panic

```xiom
// 1. Out of memory (no recovery possible)
fn alloc(size: Int) -> *Void {
  let ptr = raw_alloc(size);
  if ptr == null { panic("out of memory"); }
  return ptr;
}

// 2. Corrupted internal state
fn process_cache(cache: &Cache) -> Data {
  if cache.is_corrupted() {
    panic("cache corruption detected — restart required");
  }
  // ...
}

// 3. Unreachable code (should never execute)
fn get_name(variant: MyEnum) -> Str {
  match variant {
    MyEnum::A => return "A",
    _ => panic("unknown variant"),
  }
}
```

### panic with Detailed Messages
```xiom
panic("config file not found at '" + path + "' after searching: " + searched_paths.to_str());
```

